WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:02.167
-Billy, it is always
great to see you.

00:00:02.167 --> 00:00:03.269
Thank you
for coming on the show.

00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:04.470
I appreciate this.

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:05.804
-Jimmy, I am so happy
to be here.

00:00:05.804 --> 00:00:08.508
This is the most immersive Zoom
I've been a part of.

00:00:08.508 --> 00:00:10.410
[ Laughter ]

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:12.312
It feels like you guys
are really there.

00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:13.979
-No, these are real people!
-All of this stuff.

00:00:13.979 --> 00:00:15.181
-This is all real!
Yeah, we're happening!

00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:17.517
-This is incredible.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:17.517 --> 00:00:19.418
-I always love talking to you,

00:00:19.418 --> 00:00:20.887
and I was talking
to you backstage,

00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:22.955
and you said that you
had some dental work.

00:00:22.955 --> 00:00:27.560
-There was --
I don't know if anybody --

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.962
Well, I guess everybody
has experienced the last year

00:00:29.962 --> 00:00:31.564
or two years differently.

00:00:31.564 --> 00:00:35.368
I've experienced a little stress
during this period of time.

00:00:35.368 --> 00:00:37.904
And, apparently, the way that
I've gone about managing

00:00:37.904 --> 00:00:41.608
that stress is trying to grind
my teeth into powder at night.

00:00:41.608 --> 00:00:44.677
And so, I go to the dentist,
and my dentist says, she says,

00:00:44.677 --> 00:00:46.279
"Well, I think there's

00:00:46.279 --> 00:00:48.547
maybe a little something
going on down there,"

00:00:48.547 --> 00:00:51.084
which doesn't sound like
a very technical thing to me.

00:00:51.084 --> 00:00:52.752
So I thought,
"Well, there is, yeah.

00:00:52.752 --> 00:00:53.953
It's my teeth.

00:00:53.953 --> 00:00:56.556
And she says, "No, no,
the bone right there.

00:00:56.556 --> 00:00:57.990
I see a little bit of a shadow."

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:00.693
So I said, "Okay. Well,
what should we do about that?"

00:01:00.693 --> 00:01:03.463
Like, maybe take a different
picture or touch up the picture?

00:01:03.463 --> 00:01:04.897
-Yeah, let's just try it again,
yeah.

00:01:04.897 --> 00:01:06.932
Maybe the shadow will go away.
-You touch it up,

00:01:06.932 --> 00:01:08.367
you got Photoshop,
any of that stuff will work.

00:01:08.367 --> 00:01:10.936
-Yeah, of course, yeah.
-She doesn't have Photoshop,

00:01:10.936 --> 00:01:12.806
so she sends me to her brother.

00:01:12.806 --> 00:01:14.607
It's a family
dentistry practice --

00:01:14.607 --> 00:01:15.874
right around the corner,
by the way --

00:01:15.874 --> 00:01:18.344
if you need a referral,
I'll stay afterwards.

00:01:18.344 --> 00:01:20.346
[ Laughter ]
They're fantastic.

00:01:20.346 --> 00:01:22.281
Meet me afterwards.
Walking distance from here.

00:01:22.281 --> 00:01:24.750
-We're doing a podcast
afterwards talking about it.

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:27.153
-And the guy looks at the photo
with the little shadow,

00:01:27.153 --> 00:01:30.290
and he says, "I think there
may have to be an extraction."

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:33.993
Now, that's the first time I've
heard that word at the dentist.

00:01:33.993 --> 00:01:36.095
-An extraction.
-An extraction.

00:01:36.095 --> 00:01:38.131
-I think the worst I've done
is a root canal.

00:01:38.131 --> 00:01:40.166
-Yeah, I've done a root --
and this tooth already

00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:44.003
had a root canal on it so --
Yeah, it was deep territory.

00:01:44.003 --> 00:01:45.405
-So, an extraction.
-But I haven't heard

00:01:45.405 --> 00:01:47.240
the word extraction --
Well, I --

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:49.608
perhaps when I did the movie
"Charlotte Gray."

00:01:49.608 --> 00:01:51.444
I'm doing a plug
for all my films right now.

00:01:51.444 --> 00:01:53.246
[ Laughter ]
-Out on blu-ray right now.

00:01:53.246 --> 00:01:56.282
-Cate Blanchett.
That's right. She was --

00:01:56.282 --> 00:01:58.151
-The Criterion Collection
is coming out.

00:01:58.151 --> 00:02:00.386
[ Laughter ]
-Might have to have been

00:02:00.386 --> 00:02:02.322
extracted from France,
in occupied France.

00:02:02.322 --> 00:02:03.656
-That's correct.
-No, that was not

00:02:03.656 --> 00:02:05.191
what this was about.
So he says,

00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:06.925
"You're gonna need
an extraction."

00:02:06.925 --> 00:02:09.828
And I said, "All right.
Well, can we do that next week?"

00:02:09.828 --> 00:02:12.331
And he said, "Sure."
I come in, and he starts

00:02:12.331 --> 00:02:15.969
to numb me up, and he --
I was like,

00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:17.636
"How many needles
you got over there, boss?

00:02:17.636 --> 00:02:18.904
Like, it looks like a lot."
And he goes,

00:02:18.904 --> 00:02:21.407
"Well, I'm gonna have to
pull your tooth out

00:02:21.407 --> 00:02:24.744
and then scrape out
any infected bone,

00:02:24.744 --> 00:02:28.214
so I'm gonna numb you up
for about nine hours."

00:02:28.214 --> 00:02:29.815
[ Audience groans ]
"And in order to numb you up

00:02:29.815 --> 00:02:31.918
for nine hours,
it could take about 45 minutes

00:02:31.918 --> 00:02:36.288
for me to stick needles in there
to shoot your face full of --"

00:02:36.288 --> 00:02:38.091
What do they -- novocain.
-Sure.

00:02:38.091 --> 00:02:40.426
-Novocain.
And so I said, "All right.

00:02:40.426 --> 00:02:43.262
Well, that sounds awful."
[ Laughter ]

00:02:43.262 --> 00:02:45.664
And I was waiting for him to
say, "I'm gonna knock you out."

00:02:45.664 --> 00:02:46.832
He didn't say,
"I'll knock you --"

00:02:46.832 --> 00:02:49.435
He said, "Would you like
a little nitrous oxide?"

00:02:49.435 --> 00:02:52.771
And I said, "Well, I have
never had nitrous oxide."

00:02:52.771 --> 00:02:54.941
And he said, "Okay. It's fine."

00:02:54.941 --> 00:02:57.810
You just put this thing
on your nose, breathe into it,

00:02:57.810 --> 00:02:59.512
and see how you feel.

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:01.547
Some people feel a little
claustrophobic, he says.

00:03:01.547 --> 00:03:03.816
-You never had nitrous?
-Never had nitrous oxide before.

00:03:03.816 --> 00:03:06.052
Big mistake, error in judgment
on my part in life.

00:03:06.052 --> 00:03:07.553
[ Laughter ]

00:03:07.553 --> 00:03:09.389
So he pulls out
this contraption,

00:03:09.389 --> 00:03:12.324
puts it on my thing.
You know, I've got --

00:03:12.324 --> 00:03:14.761
Like, it's like the Bane mask
but it's on your nose.

00:03:14.761 --> 00:03:15.894
[ Laughter ]

00:03:15.894 --> 00:03:19.698
Like it's the worst
D.C. villain everywhere.

00:03:19.698 --> 00:03:22.769
It's like,
"Batman, it is my time."

00:03:22.769 --> 00:03:24.169
[ Laughter ]

00:03:24.169 --> 00:03:27.106
Bane nose is like [high-pitched]
"Batman, it's my time."

00:03:27.106 --> 00:03:28.641
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah. So you get --

00:03:28.641 --> 00:03:29.776
-So I got this thing on my nose.

00:03:29.776 --> 00:03:30.977
And he goes,
"Do you feel anything?"

00:03:30.977 --> 00:03:32.612
And I go, "No."
And he goes, "All right."

00:03:32.612 --> 00:03:34.514
And I see him turn
the little knob, and he goes,

00:03:34.514 --> 00:03:37.116
"All right, take a slow,
deep breath in."

00:03:37.116 --> 00:03:38.785
And I'm like --
[ Breathes deeply ]

00:03:38.785 --> 00:03:39.885
And he goes,
"Do you feel anything?"

00:03:39.885 --> 00:03:42.889
I'm like, "Well,
this sweet Caribbean air."

00:03:42.889 --> 00:03:45.658
[ Laughter ]

00:03:45.658 --> 00:03:46.926
[ Applause ]

00:03:46.926 --> 00:03:50.263
-You're off.
You're on vacation, buddy.

00:03:50.263 --> 00:03:52.965
-I'm like, "Is that a steel drum
I hear in the background?"

00:03:52.965 --> 00:03:54.233
[ Laughter ]

00:03:54.233 --> 00:03:56.802
"Let's get a piña colada
and get this party started."

00:03:56.802 --> 00:03:59.671
-What were you like?
-I was chatty.

00:03:59.671 --> 00:04:02.507
So, he and I talked a lot
because it took the 45 minutes

00:04:02.507 --> 00:04:03.809
to get my mouth all numb,

00:04:03.809 --> 00:04:05.978
and I learned that he was
a wrestler in college.

00:04:05.978 --> 00:04:08.014
[ Laughter ]

00:04:08.014 --> 00:04:10.182
Which I actually took
great comfort in,

00:04:10.182 --> 00:04:11.750
because I wrestled
in high school,

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:13.319
and it was really hard,
so I thought, "If you did it

00:04:13.319 --> 00:04:14.754
in college,
you must be accomplished."

00:04:14.754 --> 00:04:16.255
-Yeah.
-It came in handy

00:04:16.255 --> 00:04:19.258
45 minutes later when he's got
his foot on my chest

00:04:19.258 --> 00:04:21.460
trying to rip out my tooth.
-Oof.

00:04:21.460 --> 00:04:23.763
-The funny thing --
This is the funny thing

00:04:23.763 --> 00:04:25.998
about getting a tooth out.
Well, with me anyway.

00:04:25.998 --> 00:04:27.399
Mine didn't want to come out.

00:04:27.399 --> 00:04:29.835
You know, it's been in there
48 years or however long.

00:04:29.835 --> 00:04:32.705
It was stubborn, and I got to
see him trying to work on it,

00:04:32.705 --> 00:04:34.774
and I'm on the nitrous,
so everything's fun, you know?

00:04:34.774 --> 00:04:36.342
[ Laughter ]

00:04:36.342 --> 00:04:37.510
-And you're seeing that angle.

00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:39.312
-I'm like, "You're having
trouble, aren't you now?

00:04:39.312 --> 00:04:41.180
Suckah."
[ Laughter ]

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:42.515
-And he's like,
"No, you're the --"

00:04:42.515 --> 00:04:44.917
-He's like,
"I don't want to explain it."

00:04:44.917 --> 00:04:47.086
He should have just turned off
the nitrous at that point.

00:04:47.086 --> 00:04:53.125
So he pulls it out, and that was
not the end of the procedure.

00:04:53.125 --> 00:04:54.861
-No.
-Okay.

00:04:54.861 --> 00:04:56.328
It turns out,
when they pull it out,

00:04:56.328 --> 00:05:00.032
your whole, like, skull,
that's a resonant chamber,

00:05:00.032 --> 00:05:02.534
so you don't just feel it,
but you hear.

00:05:02.534 --> 00:05:03.970
So you hear --
[ Audience groans ]

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:05.138
Any of the vocalists over there,

00:05:05.138 --> 00:05:08.140
they know your skull
is a resonant chamber.

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:11.076
So I had that reverberating
through my ears

00:05:11.076 --> 00:05:13.479
while he starts then drilling
with his hands.

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:16.549
-Whoo!
-So I'm now a bionic man.

00:05:16.549 --> 00:05:22.454
I've got a metal -- I'll show
the camera right over here.

00:05:22.454 --> 00:05:27.126
-Camera, yeah. Camera one?
-Are you guys ready for this?

00:05:27.126 --> 00:05:28.528
[ Audience groans ]
-Wow.

00:05:28.528 --> 00:05:30.863
Wait.
What is that?

00:05:30.863 --> 00:05:34.333
-That's a titanium implant
at the moment.

00:05:34.333 --> 00:05:36.569
[ Laughter ]
Oh, you mean --

00:05:36.569 --> 00:05:38.805
Oh, you mean behind that?
That's a gold tooth, Jimmy.

00:05:38.805 --> 00:05:41.541
You don't have any gold teeth?
-Wow!

00:05:41.541 --> 00:05:44.009
Dude, you are like --
You're like Johnny Depp.

00:05:44.009 --> 00:05:45.544
You're like
"Pirates of the Caribbean."

00:05:45.544 --> 00:05:47.680
-Get yourself a gold tooth.
-Yeah.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:49.248
-So this -- Yeah,
this will stay in here.

00:05:49.248 --> 00:05:51.383
Because, also, they have
to do a bone graft.

00:05:51.383 --> 00:05:53.118
"Where does it come from?"
That's my question.

00:05:53.118 --> 00:05:55.254
-Bone-graft material?
-Yeah, I got it in my jaw now,

00:05:55.254 --> 00:05:56.989
and it only occurred to me
afterwards that I've got

00:05:56.989 --> 00:05:58.891
somebody else's bone
in my jaw now.

00:05:58.891 --> 00:06:00.759
-Wow!
-Yeah, like, could it be

00:06:00.759 --> 00:06:02.695
an animal or...
-It could be.

00:06:02.695 --> 00:06:04.330
-Yeah, well, so anyway,
I got to go back to him.

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:05.531
He's gonna pull out
that part of it.

00:06:05.531 --> 00:06:08.200
That's exactly the look
I had on my face.

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:12.238
-I had a bone graft of a --
-So, what kind of bone?

00:06:12.238 --> 00:06:16.408
-Like a pit bull.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:16.408 --> 00:06:18.477
-I feel like there's somebody
here who knows better.

00:06:18.477 --> 00:06:20.412
-No, it really is.
-It was a pit bull?

00:06:20.412 --> 00:06:22.081
We got a pit bull?
-Feel it. You can feel it.

00:06:22.081 --> 00:06:23.082
[ Barks ]
-Oh, my --

00:06:23.082 --> 00:06:25.818
[ Laughter, cheers, applause ]

00:06:25.818 --> 00:06:29.455
-Hook, line -- Hook, line,
and sinker right there, buddy.

00:06:29.455 --> 00:06:31.990
Hook, line, and sinker.

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:33.859
[ Laughs ]

00:06:33.859 --> 00:06:38.397
Hey, um, you --
Hook, line, and sinker, buddy.

00:06:38.397 --> 00:06:40.499
Hey, you won the Emmy last year.

00:06:40.499 --> 00:06:41.901
Congratulations, by the way.
-Well --

00:06:41.901 --> 00:06:43.435
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:43.435 --> 00:06:45.605
And I would give you every award
if I could.

00:06:45.605 --> 00:06:48.774
-No need to mention it.
I feel pretty confident

00:06:48.774 --> 00:06:51.844
that's what most of us
will remember 2024.

00:06:51.844 --> 00:06:54.780
-Yeah, no.
This is -- you did win the Emmy.

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:57.884
You are phenomenal in this,
and I love the whole show.

00:06:57.884 --> 00:07:00.219
-Thank you.
-I love the whole plot of it.

00:07:00.219 --> 00:07:03.356
I love the whole story line
of it, but when you came on,

00:07:03.356 --> 00:07:05.324
you just -- your acting
is so good,

00:07:05.324 --> 00:07:06.859
it's like you're not even
reading lines,

00:07:06.859 --> 00:07:08.627
it's like you're making
these lines up.

00:07:08.627 --> 00:07:10.696
I go, "Did he write this?"
I mean, you're phenomenal.

00:07:10.696 --> 00:07:12.565
-It is the craziest part
I ever read.

00:07:12.565 --> 00:07:15.267
It was so exciting,
and I feel so grateful

00:07:15.267 --> 00:07:19.004
to Kerry Ehrin and Mimi Leder
and Jen and Reese

00:07:19.004 --> 00:07:21.006
and everybody
who allowed me to do it.

00:07:21.006 --> 00:07:22.608
It was a really
once-in-a-lifetime --

00:07:22.608 --> 00:07:25.244
I'm old enough to know
how lucky I am

00:07:25.244 --> 00:07:27.547
when you get a part like that,
and I knew I was --

00:07:27.547 --> 00:07:30.316
I do my best not to screw it up.
-You are so good.

00:07:30.316 --> 00:07:33.619
I love the --
My favorite scene was you

00:07:33.619 --> 00:07:36.121
and Alex -- Jennifer Aniston...
-Yes.

00:07:36.121 --> 00:07:38.090
-...singing
"Nothing's Gonna Harm You."

00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:40.059
-Because you're a song-and-dance
man yourself, aren't ya?

00:07:40.059 --> 00:07:42.561
-When I'm around --
It was just good and, just,

00:07:42.561 --> 00:07:43.862
I loved it, man.
You're great.

00:07:43.862 --> 00:07:46.966
What's happening in season two?
-Well, season two,

00:07:46.966 --> 00:07:49.835
I think, basically --
Well, we started to shoot,

00:07:49.835 --> 00:07:52.171
and then, we had to shut down,

00:07:52.171 --> 00:07:55.474
because we started shooting
in February out in Los Angeles.

00:07:55.474 --> 00:07:57.476
-Oh, wow.
-And then, things got

00:07:57.476 --> 00:08:00.312
pretty serious, and so,
they shut down production.

00:08:00.312 --> 00:08:01.914
I flew back to New York.

00:08:01.914 --> 00:08:07.286
And they rewrote the entire
season to try to, you know,

00:08:07.286 --> 00:08:09.054
write about what was happening
in real time.

00:08:09.054 --> 00:08:13.359
And the season takes place
right after Alex and Bradley

00:08:13.359 --> 00:08:16.595
have disrupted the network
and said,

00:08:16.595 --> 00:08:18.297
"We've had enough of this.
We need a change

00:08:18.297 --> 00:08:21.633
in the power structure,"
and so, season two is about

00:08:21.633 --> 00:08:24.937
how they put the pieces back
together during a pandemic.

00:08:24.937 --> 00:08:26.472
-Yep.
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:08:26.472 --> 00:08:29.508
Here's Billy Crudup in the new
season of "The Morning Show."

00:08:29.508 --> 00:08:30.976
Take a look at this.

00:08:30.976 --> 00:08:35.348
-That was pirate television, and
it has no place on our network.

00:08:35.348 --> 00:08:38.517
-This really is
The Land that Time Forgot.

00:08:38.517 --> 00:08:41.687
All right. Well, I did my best,
but I cannot drag you idiots

00:08:41.687 --> 00:08:44.089
kicking and screaming
into the 21st century.

00:08:44.089 --> 00:08:46.458
You're just so caught up
in ruling over

00:08:46.458 --> 00:08:48.326
your rotten little fiefdom
that you don't even see

00:08:48.326 --> 00:08:50.696
the world that has
sprouted up all around you!

00:08:50.696 --> 00:08:53.999
All right. Well, enjoy
broadcasting your cave paintings

00:08:53.999 --> 00:08:56.001
to the last remaining savages
who are still watching

00:08:56.001 --> 00:08:57.903
over-the-air broadcasting.
The rest of the world,

00:08:57.903 --> 00:08:59.271
they've moved to the cloud,

00:08:59.271 --> 00:09:02.341
and it is [bleep]
gorgeous up there!

00:09:02.341 --> 00:09:04.009
-Cory, if you'll excuse us,

00:09:04.009 --> 00:09:06.645
we have a television network
we need to fix.

00:09:06.645 --> 00:09:08.613
-Well, get out that superglue.

00:09:08.613 --> 00:09:10.849
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Yeah. That's how you do it!

00:09:10.849 --> 00:09:12.218
Billy Crudup, everybody!

00:09:12.218 --> 00:09:14.220
The new season
of "The Morning Show"

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:16.856
premieres tomorrow
on Apple TV+.

